For the electrocatalysis of these gas-involving reactions, the electrocatalysts should be able to ensure fast electron and mass transport, and provide abundant tri-phase regions for surface reaction.2 With these considerations, carbon materials, with high electrical conductivity and tunable porous structure, can be promising candidates to build highly active oxygen/hydrogen electrocatalysts. State-of-the-art active materials for oxygen and hydrogen electrocatalysis are precious metals and their compounds (Pt for ORR and HER, $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ for OER, etc.). Due to the limited reserves and high cost of precious metals, non-precious metal-based materials were investigated as their alternatives for electrocatalysis.4–9 For these electrocatalysts with metal-based active sites, carbon materials can be used as multifunctional supports to enhance charge and mass transport.10,11 In the past decade, carbon materials themselves were also demonstrated to be active materials for ORR,12–15 OER,16 HER17 and multifunctional electrocatalysis.18,19 With advantages in both structural features and intrinsic activity, carbon materials serving as catalyst supports and active materials have both been widely investigated in ORR/OER/HER electrocatalysis in recent years.20,21
