Common synthesis routines of carbon materials include the exfoliation of graphite and graphite-like materials,22,23 polymerization of small molecules,24,25 pyrolysis of carboncontaining materials, etc.26,27 Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of materials constructed by connecting metal ions or clusters with organic linkers, exhibiting open crystalline structure, exceptional porosity, structural flexibility, and tunable functionality.28–30 Since the use of MOFs as precursors/ templates for synthesis of porous carbons was first reported in 2008,31 the pyrolysis of MOFs has been widely adopted as an effective way to obtain carbon-based materials with various morphologies and compositions.32,33 For the use in ORR/OER/ HER electrocatalysis, carbon-based materials derived from MOFs are featured with the following advantages: (1) the morphology and porosity of the carbon-based materials can be regulated to expose the active sites and facilitate the mass transport in electrocatalytic reactions; (2) the well dispersed metal species in carbon-based materials can be achieved to improve utilization efficiency, due to the even distribution of metal centers at atomic level in MOF precursors. (3) The incorporation of
